either open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,16).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5eaa4f2-1716-59e3-8fea-3c8121d21566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313371} {:answer/id #uuid "ef53dfc8-200c-5474-b7e1-c64b5a1d82fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313372} {:answer/id #uuid "29f023d2-2064-5cdc-a5ef-c058e30d6f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313373} {:answer/id #uuid "cff2b96b-dbed-5280-9268-b4aadcd1deba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313374} {:answer/id #uuid "35b76b0f-46cf-5b09-971f-900e6e0dd695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef53dfc8-200c-5474-b7e1-c64b5a1d82fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313372} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae6c39a-b585-5e84-b961-eddb4396d8ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "515e62a9-8010-5981-a943-f92eefe22e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105431" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 6,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<{y}^{2}<16}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,-2\\le x\\le 2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,\\sqrt{3}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7f2a38c-db13-56bd-a7ac-97c33ae47634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313378} {:answer/id #uuid "ec164832-e720-548e-991e-8ff1af22f197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313379} {:answer/id #uuid "f753d51b-3851-5f32-b257-f3b6fbe4f575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313380} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2f51fb-2346-52bd-9e2c-6e112ce40eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313381} {:answer/id #uuid "55c7ce74-344d-5b69-a062-90cbe219484e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7f2a38c-db13-56bd-a7ac-97c33ae47634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313378} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4af57d-bf2d-508e-b45f-b4a90b63bc0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "798443af-ac25-5d31-b66a-a764d670f2c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105437" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x+y<4,\\,-1<x<4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x<4,\\,2<y\\le 4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,2<y<4}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(-1,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-p